"Shows a quantity that remains constant between observations and changes at discrete effective times.\n\nUse when: Prices, inventory levels, or policies persist until the next recorded change. Linear interpolation would misrepresent what happens between measurements.\n\nAvoid when: The underlying quantity varies continuously and intermediate values are meaningful.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes time. Horizontal segments encode held values; vertical jumps encode discrete changes at chosen boundaries.\n\nBuild: Determine whether changes take effect before or after each timestamp. Sort effective times and extend horizontal segments according to that rule. Mark jumps and state the boundary convention in the chart note.\n\nCheck: Check each jump occurs at the actual effective timestamp. Verify the final held segment ends at the declared observation window. Confirm repeated timestamps are resolved rather than drawn in arbitrary order.\n\nPitfalls: A midpoint step convention can imply changes before they happened. Missing updates are not proof that the value stayed constant indefinitely.\n\nExample: Display synthetic subscription prices, holding each price from its effective date until the next recorded change.\n\nAlternatives: Line chart, Event raster, Range bar chart."
